    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d880">

                <pb facs="1118" n="1106" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg1106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">892.014/12–2146: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Siam</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Stanton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-12-31T19:00:00-05:00">December 31, 1946—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>977. ReDeptel 969, Dec 26. Prince Wan informed Dept he returning Bangkok<anchor
                        n="31" xml:id="d880-f1"/> immediately negotiate establishment Conciliation
                    Commission with French Chargé.</p>
                <p>Sent to Bangkok. Repeated to Paris and London.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d880-f1" n="31" xml:id="d880-f1-content">
                <p>On December 30. In a memorandum of that date, Mr. Landon stated that Prince Wan’s
                    list of names which his Government intended to propose as possible neutral
                    members of the Conciliation Commission contained those of two Americans—William
                    Phillips and Joseph C. Grew (892.014/12–3046).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
